{"step_index":1133,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:45:09Z","thinking":"**Examining the implications of f(c) = 2**\n\nChecking the ongoing script execution. Focusing on the case where a vertex 'c' has a function value of 2.\nConsidering the \"ball\" around 'c', specifically within a distance of 1.  Vertex 'c' is within its own ball, and the ball contains all of 'c's neighbors.\n\n\n**Analyzing vertex distances in V2**\n\nVertices not in the ball of 'c' are those not equal to 'c' and not adjacent to 'c'. Considering such a vertex 'x', it could have f(x) = 0 if dominated by a node 'u' in V2 excluding 'c'.  This implies 'x' is adjacent to 'u', and the distance from 'x' to 'c' is bounded by the distance from 'u' to 'c' plus 1.\n\nThe distance between two vertices in V2 can be at most 3 in the context of the domination chains.  Considering a path between two V2 vertices, intermediate vertices with a function value of 0 can be dominated by at most a distance of 3 to the another V2 vertex.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"/tmp/agyhome.NWyWnj/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/0d77a839-cb15-4b7b-9247-e4cbcb2ae788/.system_generated/logs/transcript.jsonl\"","ContentOffset":"328000","toolAction":"\"View Worker 4 transcript tail\"","toolSummary":"\"View Worker 4 transcript tail\""}}]}
